I just want to give a little background  on on cavalcade because I think for many  people it occupies a very special place  in cowards work for I think most of us  now who look at his work we tend to  think of him being more about  drawing-room comedy and I think  something like cavalcade seems to come  out of left field in his career but in  fact as his career was unfolding it made  a lot of sense and it figured in with a  lot of what he was already doing and as  he'd been developing in the theater up  to that time he was absolutely at the  height of his fame he had done of just  the year before private lives one of his  all-time greatest plays one of his  legendary partnerships with Gertrude  Lawrence which really defined him but he  really wanted to test himself on a  massive scale and he was playing with  all kinds of wild ideas of things he  could do and you know for those of you  who are Downton Abbey fans this story is  nothing new this was he was this was the  original upstairs downstairs in fact of  focusing on these two upper class and  working class families over the course  of 30 years and British history that  everybody knew at that time but what had  prepared him for doing something on this  scale was his work in reviews which a  lot of people forget about because he  was also staging his work as well as  usually writing the songs and and the  book and the sketches for the reviews  and a lot of his work up in that that  time had been very heavily influenced as  many people's was by the German  expressionist movement and particularly  what had happened in cinema up to that  time at a lot of it was really inspired  by that so you know well a lot of the  reviews were about having a great time  and song-and-dance numbers in comedy  some of the numbers were very dark and  often surrealistic in in how they were  staged but cavalcade was something else  again and when he finally started to  work on it they had to actually set an  opening date before he had ac